===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|tract}}, {{m|enm|tracte}}, {{m|enm|traht|t=a treatise, exposition, commentary}}, from {{inh|en|ang|traht}}, {{m|ang|tract|t=a treatise, exposition, commentary, text, passage}}; and also from {{inh|en|enm|tract}}, {{m|enm|tracte|t=an expanse of space or time}}; both from {{der|en|la|tractus|t=a haul, drawing, a drawing out}}, the perfect passive participle of {{m|la|trahō}}. {{doublet|en|trait}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# An [[area]] or [[expanse]].
#: {{ux|en|an unexplored '''tract''' of sea}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=1|passage=the deep '''tract''' of hell|year=1873}}
#* {{RQ:Addison Italy
|passage=a very high mountain joined to the mainland by a narrow '''tract''' of earth}}
#* {{RQ:Fuller Worthies of England|passage=small '''tracks''' of ground}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1951|month=October|author=R. S. McNaught|title=Lines of Approach|journal=Railway Magazine|page=703|text=Another place where, from the aesthetic point of view, a long tunnel would have been a real blessing, is East London as viewed from the carriage window on the old Great Eastern line. Despite a vast change from crowded slums to '''tracts''' of wasteland, due to its grim wartime experience, this approach still provides a shabby and unworthy introduction to the great capital.}}
# {{lb|en|anatomy}} A series of connected body organs, such as the [[digestive tract]].
# A small [[booklet]] such as a [[pamphlet]], often for promotional or informational uses.
# A brief [[treatise]] or [[discourse]] on a subject.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1731|author=w:Jonathan Swift|title=[[s:The Works of the Rev. Jonathan Swift/Volume 5/The Presbyterians Plea of Merit|The Presbyterians Plea of Merit]]
|passage=The church clergy at that writ the best collection of '''tracts''' against popery that ever appeared.}}
# A commentator's view or [[perspective]] on a subject.
# Continued or [[protracted]] [[duration]], [[length]], [[extent]]
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=5|passage=improved by '''tract''' of time|year=1873}}
#* {{RQ:Carlyle Past and Present|2|ch. XIV, Henry of Essex||Nay, in another case of litigation, the unjust Standard bearer, for his own profit, asserting that the cause belonged not to St. Edmund’s Court, but to ''his'' in [[:w:Leyland (hundred)|Lailand Hundred]], involved us in travellings and innumerable expenses, vexing the servants of St. Edmund for a long '''tract''' of time {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|Roman Catholicism}} Part of the [[proper]] of the [[liturgical]] [[celebration]] of the [[Eucharist]] for many [[Christian]] [[denomination]]s, used instead of the [[alleluia]] during [[Lenten]] or pre-Lenten seasons, in a Requiem Mass, and on a few other [[penitential]] occasions.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Continuity or extension of anything.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1669|author=William Holder|title=Elements of Speech
|passage=in '''tract''' of speech}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Traits; features; lineaments.
#* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|chapter=Of Simulation and Dissimulation|passage=The discovery of a man's self by the '''tracts''' of his countenance is a great weakness.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The footprint of a wild animal.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Metamorphoses|13|passage=The Prophet Telemus {{...}}mark'd the '''Tracts''' of every Bird that flew}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Track; trace.
#* {{RQ:Browne Pseudodoxia Epidemica|edition=2nd|passage=Efface all '''tract''' of its traduction.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Timon of Athens|text=But flies an eagle flight, bold, and forth on, / Leaving no '''tract''' behind.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Treatment; exposition.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 8|I|I|text=The '''tract''' of every thing Would, by a good discourser, lose some life Which action's self was tongue to.}}

